   168	## System
   169	
   170	Literal high-`z` depth `d` means using
   171	`Gbar` bands `z^27,...,z^(28-d)`.  A monomial `y^i z^j` in `Gbar` has local
   172	order
   173	
   174	```text
   175	q=-2i+5j+9.
   176	```
   177	
   178	Only terms with `0<=q<=d` were kept.  The `q=0` terms are fixed by
   179	`p(pi)^9=pi^9(pi^2-1)^9`.  For example:
   180	
   181	```text
   182	z^27:  y^72 z^27
   183	z^25: -9 y^67 z^25
   184	z^23:  36 y^62 z^23
   185	```
   186	
   187	The `Fbar` bands are induced recursively from the approximate-root relation
   188	`Gbar^2-Fbar^3=0` in the corresponding high `z` bands:
   189	
   190	```text
   191	F_18=y^48,
   192	3*y^96*F_(18-r) = [z^(54-r)](Gbar^2 - previously known Fbar^3).
   193	```
   194	
   195	Every approximate-root division had zero remainder in the built systems.
   196	The Jacobian rows are the coefficients of
   197	
   198	```text
   199	J_{y,z}(Fbar,Gbar)=F_y G_z - F_z G_y
   200	```
   201	
   202	in the visible high `z` bands.  Since the Jacobian is a constant, all these
   203	positive `z` bands must vanish.
   204	
   205	Singular rings used `dp` order and variables in the declared order printed in
   206	each `.sing` file:
   207	
   208	```text
   209	ring R=char,(Gbar-coefficients,gamma,split_c,T),dp;
   210	ideal I=Eq, split_c-1, T*gamma*split_c-1;
   211	```
   212	
   213	Controls in every run:
   214	
   215	```text
   216	ideal(split_c,T*gamma*split_c-1)              -> unit
   217	ideal(split_c-1,gamma-1,T*gamma*split_c-1)    -> nonunit
   218	ideal(Eq,split_c-1)                           -> nonunit
   219	```
   220	
   221	No bare `sat()` was used.
